Chapter: HostedServe
  cites Works chapter Http
  cites Works chapter WebRoute
  cites Foreword chapter CCE

 Serving HTTP on a HOSTED target, over the host's TCP.

 `web-serve` and its neighbours are not available here and that is the whole
 reason this chapter exists: every one of them builds its session with
 `net-session-new` over a NIC, and a hosted binary has no NIC below it. What it
 has instead is `host-socket`, which is the host's own sockets, so the byte
 stream arrives already sequenced and nothing here reaches `NetworkStack`,
 `TcpTransport` or a driver.

 THE ROUTE FUNCTION IS UNCHANGED. `hosted-serve` takes the same
 `(HttpRequest -> HttpResponse)` the bare-metal server takes, dispatches it
 through the same `web-dispatch`, and answers the same standard endpoints. An
 app that compiles for both targets writes one route function and changes only
 which serve it calls.

 The selector values are the ones the emitter names in
 `codex/compiler/Emit/X86_64Boot.codex`. A program reaches the builtin rather
 than the compiler's constants, so they are spelled again here.

 We say:

Section: Socket Operations

  hs-op-socket : Integer = 0
  hs-op-bind : Integer = 1
  hs-op-listen : Integer = 2
  hs-op-accept : Integer = 3
  hs-op-close : Integer = 4
  hs-op-recv : Integer = 5
  hs-op-send : Integer = 6
  hs-op-reuseaddr : Integer = 7

 One request's headers and body. The same 64 KiB the bare-metal server caps a
 receive at, for the same reason: it is one request, not a stream budget.

  hs-buffer-bytes : Integer = 65536
  hs-backlog : Integer = 16

 What `hosted-listen` answers when a call refuses, one value per call, so a
 caller reports WHICH of the three refused rather than a bare negative.

 The three are named rather than written as `(0 - 2)` at the point of use for a
 second reason, learned from CDX1070: a parenthesised expression cannot end an
 `act` block that follows an application, because the parser reads the `(` on
 the next line as a further argument to the application above.

  hs-err-socket : Integer = 0 - 1
  hs-err-bind : Integer = 0 - 2
  hs-err-listen : Integer = 0 - 3

Section: Buffer and List

 `host-socket` reads and writes RAW MEMORY, and `http-parse-request` and
 `http-encode` speak `List Integer`. These two walks are the whole of the
 marshalling, and they are separate from the loop so that a change to either
 side is one edit.

 The bytes on the wire are UNICODE octets, not CCE, exactly as they are for the
 bare-metal server: `http-parse-request` and `http-encode` own that conversion,
 so nothing here converts anything.

  hs-buf-to-list : Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  hs-buf-to-list (buf) (i) (n) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else hs-buf-to-list buf (i + 1) n (list-push acc (peek-byte buf i))

  hs-list-to-buf : Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  hs-list-to-buf (buf) (bs) (i) (n) =
    if i >= n then n
    else let ignored = poke-byte buf i (list-at bs i)
    in hs-list-to-buf buf bs (i + 1) n

Section: Serving One Connection

 A SHORT SEND IS NOT A SENT RESPONSE. `send` answers how many bytes it took,
 and a partial write leaves the client reading a body that stops mid-stream, so
 the loop keeps offering the remainder until the whole response is gone or the
 call refuses. The bare-metal server closes on a short send for the same
 reason, stated in `WebServer`: a half-written response cannot be repaired.

  hs-send-all : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Network.Write] Integer
  hs-send-all (fd) (buf) (off) (n) = act
    if off >= n then off
    else act
      sent <- host-socket hs-op-send fd (buf + off) (n - off)
      if sent <= 0 then (0 - 1)
      else hs-send-all fd buf (off + sent) n
    end
  end

 One connection, one request, one response, then close. Keep-alive is not here
 and is not an oversight: the responses this serves carry `Connection: close`,
 and concurrency belongs with `web-serve-concurrent`'s own design.

  HostedServed = record { state : WebState, ok : Boolean }

  hs-serve-one : (HttpRequest -> HttpResponse), WebState, Integer, Integer -> [Network.Write] HostedServed
  hs-serve-one (route) (st) (cfd) (buf) = act
    n <- host-socket hs-op-recv cfd buf hs-buffer-bytes
    if n <= 0 then act
      closed <- host-socket hs-op-close cfd 0 0
      HostedServed { state = st, ok = False }
    end
    else
      let req = http-parse-request (hs-buf-to-list buf 0 n [])
      in let ns = WebState { request-count = (st.request-count) + 1 }
      in let resp = if req.valid then web-dispatch route web-observe-none ns req else http-error "Bad request"
      in let out = http-encode resp
      in let len = list-length out
      in let staged = hs-list-to-buf buf out 0 len
      in act
        sent <- hs-send-all cfd buf 0 len
        closed <- host-socket hs-op-close cfd 0 0
        HostedServed { state = ns, ok = sent >= 0 }
      end
  end

Section: The Accept Loop

 A NEGATIVE `remaining` NEVER REACHES ZERO, and that is how the unbounded form
 is spelled: it counts down past -1 and the stop test is equality, so the loop
 ends only when accept refuses. Counting down to a bound and running forever
 are then the same code rather than two loops kept in step by hand.

  hs-loop : (HttpRequest -> HttpResponse), WebState, Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Network.Write] Integer
  hs-loop (route) (st) (fd) (buf) (remaining) = act
    if remaining == 0 then (st.request-count)
    else act
      cfd <- host-socket hs-op-accept fd 0 0
      if cfd < 0 then (st.request-count)
      else act
        served <- hs-serve-one route st cfd buf
        hs-loop route (served.state) fd buf (remaining - 1)
      end
    end
  end

 `hosted-serve-n` stops after `count` requests and `hosted-serve` runs until
 accept refuses. The bounded form is what a test can drive to completion; the
 unbounded one is what an application calls.

 LISTENING AND SERVING ARE SEPARATE ENTRY POINTS, and the separation earns its
 keep in the harness rather than in an application. A test has to know the
 server reached `listen` before the test connects, and a readiness probe that
 dials the port and hangs up is answered by `accept` and consumed as the one
 connection the server was waiting for. Splitting the two lets a caller print a
 line of its own between `hosted-listen` and `hosted-serve-on`, which costs no
 connection. `hosted-listen` answers a descriptor, or a negative naming the
 call that refused: -1 socket, -2 bind, -3 listen.

  hosted-listen : Integer -> [Network.Write] Integer
  hosted-listen (port) = act
    fd <- host-socket hs-op-socket 0 0 0
    if fd < 0 then hs-err-socket
    else act
      reused <- host-socket hs-op-reuseaddr fd 0 0
      bound <- host-socket hs-op-bind fd port 0
      if bound < 0 then act
        closed <- host-socket hs-op-close fd 0 0
        hs-err-bind
      end
      else act
        listening <- host-socket hs-op-listen fd hs-backlog 0
        if listening < 0 then act
          closed <- host-socket hs-op-close fd 0 0
          hs-err-listen
        end
        else fd
      end
    end
  end

  hosted-serve-on : (HttpRequest -> HttpResponse), Integer, Integer -> [Network.Write] Integer
  hosted-serve-on (route) (fd) (count) =
    let buf = alloc-bytes hs-buffer-bytes
    in act
      served <- hs-loop route web-new fd buf count
      closed <- host-socket hs-op-close fd 0 0
      served
    end

  hosted-serve-n : (HttpRequest -> HttpResponse), Integer, Integer -> [Network.Write] Integer
  hosted-serve-n (route) (port) (count) = act
    fd <- hosted-listen port
    if fd < 0 then fd
    else hosted-serve-on route fd count
  end

  hosted-serve : (HttpRequest -> HttpResponse), Integer -> [Network.Write] Integer
  hosted-serve (route) (port) = hosted-serve-n route port (0 - 1)
